 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Improves high-frequency details without distorting real textures [S1]. Effective when combined with patch-based MSE to handle misalignment [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires pre-aligned ground truth patches [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When optimizing neural radiance fields or similar renderers where high-frequency detail recovery is critical and misalignment is handled via patch-based alignment [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** Not given in the sources [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the forward pass through the first block of VGG-19, which is lightweight compared to the full network.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing intermediate feature maps from the first VGG block for the gradient computation.
+*   **Strengths:** Improves high-frequency details while preserving real textures, addressing the smoothness issues of pixel-wise MSE losses [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to misalignment between ground truth and rendered patches [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When optimizing radiance fields or images where high-frequency detail recovery is critical and standard MSE loss results in overly smooth outputs [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When computational resources are extremely limited or when the input images are heavily misaligned without prior alignment correction [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The High-Frequency Aware Loss was introduced in the AligNeRF paper [S1]. It was developed to address the limitations of standard perceptual losses, which, while good at capturing high-frequency details, often distort the actual texture of objects [S1]. The authors modified the original perceptual loss proposed by Johnson et al. [12] by using only the output of the first block of VGG-19 before max-pooling [S1]. This approach was integrated into the AligNeRF training pipeline, replacing per-pixel MSE loss with a combination of patch-based MSE and this shallow VGG feature-space loss [S1].
+The High-Frequency Aware Loss was introduced in the AligNeRF paper [S1]. It modifies the original perceptual loss proposed by Johnson et al. [S1, citing [12]] by using only the output of the first block of a pre-trained VGG-19 model, before the max-pooling layer. This modification was motivated by observations in image restoration tasks where deeper perceptual losses distorted actual textures [S1]. The loss is typically combined with a patch-based MSE loss to handle misalignment, replacing the standard per-pixel MSE loss [S1].
 
